2026-10-24 T-317F: Capture normalized tool failures centrally in a machine-local SQLite ledger, with allowlisted summaries instead of raw payloads. Reuse authenticated API and Conductor tools for grouped triage and explicit remediation; never disable a tool from one failure. Keep diagnostics local because tool installations and runtime conditions differ per machine. Global prompt policy covers every role and harness; hidden inner calls require safe agent reporting. No live server restart or unsupported config edit. Source thread 317ffed2-e4bf-4748-812d-488b6dc0662a.
2026-10-28 T-TKOV: Take over a thread from another synced Mac as a request on top of Hand off: the asking Mac writes handoff.request.<thread>, the Mac that holds the thread answers with the usual offer, and the asking Mac takes it with the existing pickup. The holder between turns is handoff.holder.<thread>, written only by the Mac a thread moves to (a turn starting there, or a pickup), not a thread column, because the thread row is last-writer-wins and a rename on the other Mac would overwrite it. The holder answers only as holder and only onto a settled offer, so a stale Mac never gives an old session away; only the holder may Hand off. The asking Mac alone ends a request (cancel, one hour, or the thread moving), so no Mac compares clocks. A sleeping holder makes the request wait; forced takeover, checkpoints and crew threads stay out. Source threads 0cdcc478 (plan) and fdf85080-5a5d-427a-8f8f-aaa2cc390fe7.
2026-10-30 T-399: One execution owner per thread: an `assignments` row (machine, generation, routed) that every turn, pickup and routing claims, synced by generation so a late copy of an old owner never wins. The live registry derives from it; `run_machine` stays a status-write tag; `handoff.holder` and `runon.thread` are kept only for older builds. The check moved to the delivery boundary: `turnBlocked` for Ben's replies, `wakeBlocked` for Omni's own wakes (which never move a thread), applied in the runner's `wake`. Generation fencing stops a stale turn after sync. A disconnected machine may start work on what it holds and nothing else; no lease expires on its own. "Remote provider" (Hermes, Cursor Cloud: no local files) is named apart from "remote machine" (another synced Mac). Issue #399.
2026-10-31 T-410: One lifecycle owner per thread process: `runner/controller.ts` (ThreadController) is the only writer of a process's state; every other runner module reads it as `Live`, with each field read-only, and moves it through the controller's methods, so typecheck fails on a write elsewhere. The runner reaches run-on, handoff and publishing only through `runner/ports.ts`, bound once in `apps/server/compose.ts`, which also builds each controller's reporting, scheduling and persistence; `tests/import-boundaries.test.ts` fails on any runtime import cycle that crosses an owner. The states are drawn in docs/internals/execution-state.md. Issue #410.
2026-11-01 T-CONNECT: Omni Connect follows T3 Connect on the user's own relay, one user per relay. A machine signed in to sync registers with its relay token; the relay, given CF_API_TOKEN, CF_ACCOUNT_ID, CF_ZONE_ID and CONNECT_ZONE, provisions a Cloudflare tunnel and a proxied <name>-<6 hex>.omni-os.app CNAME per machine, and the server runs the cloudflared on PATH (or OMNI_CLOUDFLARED) with the connector token. The request guard accepts the machine's own Connect hostname and still requires the token or a session there, without the Tailscale login check. The relay's /connect page signs in once with Clerk's device grant; opening a machine has the relay sign a mint request that the machine verifies against the relay key it pinned, answered with a one-time code bound to a key made on the machine's origin, which the browser redeems with a proof of that key for an ordinary device session. Turned down: a hosted cross-origin Web UI, per-machine Google sign-in over bare tunnels, Cloudflare Access, and T3's push, reaping and pinned-download extras. ADR 0009.
2026-11-01 T-UPDATE: Omni runs like T3 Code: the desktop app owns the server, so opening the app starts it and quitting stops it, and there is no LaunchAgent (scripts/install-launchd.sh is gone; the Mac app boots out an old com.omni-os.server agent and renames its plist to .plist.disabled; the server runs caffeinate -i -w itself on a Mac). Updating is one Update button. The server fetches origin's main every 15 minutes and compares it with the commit it booted from (GET /api/update, a feed `update` event). Update waits for running turns, runs scripts/sync-main.sh, rebuilds the Web UI and exits with code 75; the app that started it (OMNI_SUPERVISOR=mac or linux) starts it again, so a server without one shows the notice but no button. The Mac app's own button also rebuilds and relaunches the app when the app is behind. The Conductor reaches it as omni_update.
2026-11-02 T-DESKTOP: The desktop app is one Electron shell on the Web UI, as T3 Code's is: apps/desktop loads apps/web unchanged in framework and reaches the OS through one bridge (window.omniDesktop, typed in packages/shared/desktop-bridge.ts, absent in a browser or on a phone). The server is bundled (apps/server/scripts/bundle.ts) and runs on Electron's own Node with ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 and OMNI_SUPERVISOR=desktop from a staged folder that mirrors the repo's layout, so the app needs no checkout and no system Node; it attaches to this install's server or starts one, and restarts it after exit 75. It ships a DMG and zip for Mac arm64, x64 and universal and a Linux AppImage, so apps/linux folds into it. The SwiftUI app is replaced, then deleted once Ben has used the new app daily; native SwiftUI controls, ADR 0002's reason, are what is given up. Spike: Electron 44.10.1 bundles Node 24.22.1, node:sqlite loads under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE, node-pty is N-API with darwin prebuilds; Keychain calls from the Electron-Node child are still to be checked on a Mac. The third surface's QA harness is apps/desktop/scripts/qa.ts (Playwright's Electron driver), which refuses port 4747. Supersedes ADR 0002 and 0006. ADR 0010.
2026-11-09 T-ARTSCREEN: Ben's claude.ai Artifacts list comes from the claude CLI's own Artifact tool: a one-off Haiku run (`claude -p --tools Artifact`, `--setting-sources project`, no session) with a PreToolUse guard that lets only an Artifact `list` through, parsed from the tool results (never the model's reply), with a listing accepted only when its rows match its own header count. Failures (cli_missing, signed_out, tool_unavailable, limit, format_changed, timeout, failed) keep the last good list. The cache is machine-local. Rows are keyed by uuid: the short `claude.ai/artifact/<id>` is the uuid in base58, so both link forms join. Refresh on open after 10 minutes, Refresh always, a Daily (default), 6-hourly or Off schedule; type badges mapped once a day. Rows open outside Omni (new tab, shell.openExternal, Linking.openURL). Turned down: scraping or a session cookie, a thread per refresh, model-written JSON, types every run, offline HTML copies. ADR 0011.
2026-11-14 T-PRD667: Machines sign what they push and only signed changes make a machine act. Each machine keeps an Ed25519 key (private half in its secret store, public half as `signKey` in its synced device record next to `sealKey`); every change carries `signer` and `sig` over entity, id, payload hash and timestamp; the relay binds `machine_id` to the token that pushed. Peers' keys are pinned on first sight and change only when Ben confirms a new key in Settings > Sync. `remote_command`, `assignment`, `update.ask.*`, `automations.owner` and secret drops apply only with a valid signature; other entities are logged and still applied during rollout, behind a setting that turns strict later. Synced `cwd`, `repo_path` and `base_dir` must sit under home or a channel checkout and outside `.ssh`, `.gnupg` and Omni's data, signed or not. Secret drops seal only to a pinned key. The relay stays trusted for Connect sessions (ADR 0009) and can no longer forge actions from a machine another machine has pinned, but while keys are pinned on first sight it can still introduce a never-seen machine with its own key and sign action entities as it. Turned down: signing only action entities, relay-side signing, mTLS. Open for Ben: confirm-first pinning, refusing action entities from a machine Ben has never confirmed. ADR 0012, issue #667.
